    <h2>The Costs Beyond the Deposit: What You Actually Need on Settlement Day</h2>
    <p>This is where many first home buyers get caught out. The deposit is the biggest number, but it is not the only one.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Stamp duty</strong> is a state government tax on the purchase of property. In most states, first home buyers receive concessions on properties below a certain threshold. In NSW, stamp duty is waived entirely for first home buyers purchasing properties up to $800,000. In Victoria, full exemption applies for purchases up to $600,000. In Queensland, the exemption applies up to $550,000 for new builds receiving the First Home Owner Grant. For properties above the concession threshold, stamp duty can add $15,000 to $50,000+ to your upfront costs. Use <a href="/australia/stamp-duty-calculator/">CalcPhi's Stamp Duty Calculator</a> for your exact figure by state.</li>
      <li><strong>Conveyancing and legal fees</strong> typically run between $1,500 and $3,000 for a standard residential purchase.</li>
      <li><strong>Building and pest inspections</strong> cost between $400 and $800. Skipping these to save money is one of the most common and costly mistakes first home buyers make.</li>
      <li><strong>Loan application and establishment fees</strong> vary by lender — many have moved to zero application fees, but some still charge up to $600.</li>
      <li><strong>Moving costs</strong> of $500 to $2,500 depending on distance and volume.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A practical rule of thumb: budget 3% to 5% of the purchase price for total upfront costs beyond your deposit. On a $750,000 property, that means having $22,500 to $37,500 in additional cash available at settlement.</p>


